> One proposal is that we call this session variable `default_transaction_quality_of_service` and then have a separate `quality_of_service` transaction setting, so you can do `BEGIN; SET TRANSACTION qualit_of_service = 'expedited'; ...; COMMIT;` and have the subsequent transaction observe no effect. This could be nice for applications which share a connection pool and want to only prioritize or deprioritize certain transactions. I suspect it's not much harder. Curious to hear if @vy-ton or others have thoughts.

@ajwerner @vy-ton 
I'm trying to increase the chances that this can get merged before the end of February stability period deadline, so it may be good to decide soon whether we want to add `SET TRANSACTION quality_of_service`.  If we think it's a high likelihood that we would support this statement at some point (whether in this PR or later), I could change the name of the session flag now to default_transaction_quality_of_service (is this the best name?).

`default_transaction_use_follower_reads` is something of a hack if I'm being honest. I wouldn't model this on that. Part of the reason I say that is that you cannot change the timestamp of a transaction after it starts, you can however change it's `READ ONLY` nature, and, eventually, we can imagine changing it's quality of service. I don't think we need to support the more dynamic or txn scoped feature in this PR, but let's just use the terminology so we can add it later?

----

Note that `SET [SESSION] transaction_read_only = ` is equivalent to `SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY`. I validate the below have the same behavior on cockroach and postgres. Importantly, this is a transaction-state property and not a session property. It's weird that there's session variable syntax for it. In all of those places, I'm pretty certain you could replace one for the other (I tried most of them in both databases). 

In postgres;

```
postgres=# set transaction_read_only=true;
SET
postgres=# insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 2, 'b');
INSERT 0 1
```

```
postgres=# begin;
BEGIN
postgres=*# set transaction_read_only=true;
SET
postgres=*# insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 3, 'c');
2022-02-15 12:38:49.991 EST [5395] ERROR:  cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction
2022-02-15 12:38:49.991 EST [5395] STATEMENT:  insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 3, 'c'::char);
ERROR:  cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction
```

```
postgres=# begin;
BEGIN
postgres=*# insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 3, 'c');
INSERT 0 1
postgres=*# set transaction_read_only=true;
SET
postgres=*# insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 4, 'd');
2022-02-15 12:39:22.309 EST [5395] ERROR:  cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction
2022-02-15 12:39:22.309 EST [5395] STATEMENT:  insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 4, 'd');
ERROR:  cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction
```

```
postgres=# begin;
BEGIN
postgres=*# set transaction_read_only=true;
SET
postgres=*# commit;
COMMIT
postgres=# insert into foo  (a, b) values ( 4, 'd');
INSERT 0 1
```

It's funny how much the challenge here is all terminology. I discovered Cisco has: Gold/Video, Silver/Best Effort (default), and Bronze/Background

I don't think Video applies, but these medal levels would be a funny but consistent choice. I feel like it doesn't matter all that much what we choose. 

@vy-ton can you be the decider?

